$)\\,⋃\\,(${{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >12x \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-12x$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ x(x-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=12,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/387b02ad3416b06b3f86f12c99b921800ab4b86f39be9da0fc48883952d366d2.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<0$ or $x>12.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)⋃(12,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-277664/field-1" "q-277664/field-2" "q-277664/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530fc010-b15b-5877-8ed6-7fd2f8feb37c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277670/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "2213bcde-f411-52e7-89d5-75c0d9453f8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277670/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf39dc5c-a856-5b78-a9f5-723f472e4485" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277670/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-277670/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277670/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "c57b760c-2f49-5596-b1a7-e36f9eb8cb8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277670/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1481635d-90cb-50f3-a54d-91682e323b61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277670/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-277670/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277670" :question/id #uuid "e3b29a5b-bbb4-5fb4-8d0f-7a1a962e9cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-277670" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}>-x-12$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & >-x-12 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+x+12 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x+3)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/32e9a7babfbe9132a3a3e18c2f1efcbc3a33cd59bccc02099c7045b3fc39ee75.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+x+12>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3<x<4.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-3,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-277670/field-1" "q-277670/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530fc010-b15b-5877-8ed6-7fd2f8feb37c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5186"] :question/problem "Find the solution of $x^2-2x-8 \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\nx^2-2x-8 \\geq 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=x^2-2x-8\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/6e9eeff6caaad8efe92d4726c1f04175ed3023ea99b7990436597233ef2910df.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches this axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq -2$ or $x \\geq 4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9977"] :question/problem "Find the solution of $-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny = -x^2 + 7 x - 10\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-10 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-7x+10) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x = 5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/13e108b6383e99c5840789389378fd58cd8704484296485cb6483112ecf4ae25.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq 2$ or $x \\geq 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5063"] :question/problem "Find the solution of $2x^2-6 \\le -4x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6 & \\le -4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2+2x-3$ lies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=1$ and $x=-3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/0fe5ab0d38cea340e2227157bf1d434a0fccbab153289db52106857530fbc4dc.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-3 \\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis.  \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3 \\le x \\le 1.$  \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x \\in [-3,1].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9936"] :question/problem "Find the solution of $2x^2 < -4x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & <-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y = 2x^2 + 4x$ lies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x = -2$ and $x = 0,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/936653d6efd2ff155768730d61af5b68924c48933fa81b77998a668b7691fa10.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n2x^2 + 4x \\lt 0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-2 < x < 0.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x \\in \\left(-2, 0\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
